From 4f7a3ec57c3078384985877a9c3a029773033093 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 13:14:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] GX worker: cheaper draws, for a slower CPU

The game waits for the FIFO translation worker at every frame's end, so the
worker's work per draw sets how slow a core may be. A tester's Ryzen 5 5600X
got about 25 frames a second; on an i9-13900KF pinned to 12 of its efficiency
cores (slower than a Zen 3 core one thread at a time) Wind Waker's Outset
spawn view, about 14,400 draws a frame, ran at 23 to 26 game frames a second,
the game waiting 190 to 250 ms a second for the worker.

- gxcore: the pipeline key derived from the BP, CP and XF registers (TEV,
  channels, texgens, blending, depth) is cached, keyed by a version apply()
  changes whenever a BP, CP or VAT register changes value, and by the draw's
  own XF, channel, light and texture values; the vertex walk is cached by its
  VCD and VAT (from native-60hz-pc dc81247 and f863821). The host's draw tag
  registers (0x7B-0x7E), which change at nearly every particle draw and feed
  no derived state, leave the version alone. 96 percent of draws hit.
  DOL_GXCORE_DERIVED_VERIFY=1 derives the state again at every hit and
  reports a mismatch; DOL_GXCORE_DERIVED_CACHE=0 turns the cache off.
- frontend: the gxcore renderer's consumer no longer walks every vertex's
  indices for assembly totals only the shadow packet sink and the replay
  tools read (set_assembly_totals).
- Aurora: a draw's pipeline lookup is remembered for the next draw with the
  same config (one entry for the colour and one for the early-depth
  pipeline) until the pipeline cache is shut down, with no std::function
  allocation, hash or lock; a textured draw with the view and sampler state
  of the one before reuses its bind group within the frame.
- Aurora: Smooth Motion's job for a draw whose constants repeat the job
  before's carries no 2.8 KB copy (the helper keeps the last it was given),
  and the sequentially consistent fence is paid only when enough jobs wait
  that the helper may be asleep.

On the E-cores the spawn view runs at 29 to 30 game frames a second, the
game waiting 20 to 65 ms a second; unpaced with Smooth Motion off, 29.7 to
34.4, the game thread now the limit. Frames are unchanged: the same 19
capture hashes (Smooth Motion off) and 62 real and in-between frame dumps
(Smooth Motion 60) as before on Outset, and 34,851,442 verified cache hits
with no mismatch through the new-game opening.
